-- Passage rg7-text-structure-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('65087cb5-9146-5d13-b95e-1f7c555dfc2f', 'rg7-text-structure-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Two Ways to Cross a River$q$, $q$Engineers who need to carry a road across a river have many choices. Two of the oldest and most common designs are the arch bridge and the suspension bridge. Both do the same job, but they handle weight in very different ways.

An arch bridge uses a curved shape to carry its load. When a truck rolls across, its weight pushes down on the arch. The curve spreads that push outward and down into thick supports at each end. Because the stone or concrete is squeezed together, an arch bridge works by compression. Builders have used arches for thousands of years, and some very old stone arches still stand today.

A suspension bridge works the opposite way. Its road hangs from thick steel cables that drape between tall towers. The weight of traffic pulls down on the cables, stretching them tight. This pulling force is called tension. The ends of the cables are fastened into enormous blocks of concrete buried in the ground on each shore.

The two designs also suit different places. An arch bridge is sturdy and needs little upkeep, but a single arch can only stretch so far. It works best across narrow rivers and deep, rocky gorges. A suspension bridge can span much greater distances, so it is often chosen for wide bays. However, its long, light road can sway in strong winds, so engineers must stiffen it carefully.

In short, an arch pushes and a suspension bridge pulls. Knowing which force a bridge depends on helps engineers decide which design fits the river in front of them.$q$, 'draft')
