-- Passage rg7-text-structure-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1b0dfe24-709d-5d92-af6a-39ec60563caf', 'rg7-text-structure-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Cutting Food Waste at Hollis Middle School$q$, $q$Last fall, the student council at Hollis Middle School noticed something troubling. At the end of each lunch period, the trash cans were heavy with whole apples, unopened milk cartons, and half-eaten sandwiches. A one-week count showed that students were throwing away about forty kilograms of food every day.

The council members wanted to understand why. They surveyed two hundred students. Many said they took items they did not want because the lunch line moved too fast to choose. Others said they did not have time to finish eating because they rushed to get outside for recess.

The council proposed three changes. First, they set up a share table where students could leave unopened food for others to take. Second, the cafeteria began letting students pick only the sides they wanted. Third, the principal agreed to schedule recess before lunch so students would not hurry through their meals.

After two months, the council repeated its count. Daily food waste had dropped to about seventeen kilograms. Unopened milk was now rarely thrown away, and the share table emptied by the end of most days. Some students still left vegetables on their trays, so the council is now working with the cafeteria to test new recipes.

The project showed that a large problem can shrink when people first study its causes. Other schools in the district have since asked the council to share its survey.$q$, 'draft')
