-- Passage rg7-text-structure-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8e917edd-abd9-5730-a46c-7fdc69229ebf', 'rg7-text-structure-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-text-structure'), null,
        '6-8', $q$A Beginner's Guide to Stargazing$q$, $q$Many people think stargazing requires an expensive telescope. In fact, a clear night and a little planning are enough to begin. This guide explains how to get started.

Find a Dark Spot

Light from streetlights and buildings washes out faint stars. Look for a place away from bright lights, such as a park, a field, or a backyard with the porch light off. Check the weather, too, because even thin clouds can hide the sky.

Let Your Eyes Adjust

Your eyes need about twenty minutes to adjust to the dark. During that time, your pupils widen to let in more light. Looking at a bright phone screen resets the process, so keep your phone in your pocket.

Look for Patterns

Instead of trying to learn single stars, look for groups that form shapes. These patterns, called constellations, act like landmarks. Once you find one, you can use it to locate its neighbors.

Add Simple Tools

A star chart shows which constellations are visible in each season. Binoculars, which many families already own, reveal craters on the Moon and many stars too faint to see with your eyes alone.

Quick Tip: Bring a blanket and a notebook. Sketching what you see will help you find it again next time.$q$, 'draft')
